	 ('4394c6d8-8432-4152-b9f4-a3d7b887efbe'::uuid,'title:Deprecate symbols from your libraries… Why and how?, description:When you bundle third-party classes into your own module, they often leak into the public API — even though you never intended them to be public. Normally you’d relocate them to a different package, but sometimes constraints prevent this. In this talk, I’ll share how we solved this problem in the Kotlin Gradle plugin by applying bytecode transformations that deprecate bundled library symbols, discouraging their use in downstream code and the story behind. I’ll also show how the same idea can be applied to regular dependencies, giving you more control over which parts of a library are actually available to your project.','{"id": "a6c8e20fd2aa48de8b509994850cd76d", "room": "SUB 2", "title": "Deprecate symbols from your libraries… Why and how?", "endsAt": 1764248400000, "category": "[Fast Forward]", "dayIndex": 1, "speakers": "[Alexander Likhachev]", "startsAt": 1764246600000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2025-11-27", "durationMinutes": 30, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2025-11-27T14:00:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2025-11-27T13:30:00"}','[-0.016861431,0.018241003,0.028441472,0.0006366584,0.0126809105,0.034726188,-0.05858302,-0.02934725,-0.04997115,0.0010912899,0.024330627,-0.029514471,0.012437047,-0.017404899,0.0455398,0.0011313532,0.010437365,-0.00021033242,-0.0031928723,0.061871696,0.0476858,0.014199833,0.044536475,0.045093875,0.026393017,0.003811241,-0.021752639,0.025041316,0.08957461,-0.024121601,-0.00029764432,-0.01969025,-0.018394288,-0.009538554,0.027020095,-0.0067132185,0.030935848,-0.024414238,0.017613925,-0.040941227,0.0029211384,-0.10473596,-0.010102924,0.032580186,-0.047323488,0.06917367,0.01307806,-0.009650034,0.0014806009,0.00833317,-0.05640915,-0.03726237,0.057691175,0.04590211,-0.04790876,-0.044508602,-0.017112263,0.026281536,-0.010298015,0.019369744,0.059028942,-0.02285351,0.0108832875,-0.013809651,-0.07380012,-0.00217387